	affiliations = {JNTUA College of Engineering, Ananthapuramu, Department of EEE, Anantapur, AP, India; Sri Venkateswara University College Of Engineering, Department of EEE, Tirupati, AP, India},
	abstract = {The involvement of electric vehicles (EVs) and renewable distributed generators (DGs) is changing the complete dynamics of radial distribution systems. This paper investigates the impact of EV charging stations and renewable DGs, specifically solar and wind-based systems, on the performance of balanced radial distribution networks. A novel, time-varying EV charging model is proposed, reflecting practical charging behaviour, with a standardised charging gun capacity of 30 kW. Unlike existing literature, the charging of EVs at fast-charging stations is modelled across varying states of charge (SoC), with a demonstrated capability to achieve full charge within 90 min. To mitigate the additional load imposed by EV charging stations, the study integrates solar and wind-based DGs as compensatory mechanisms, optimising system reliability and voltage stability. The proposed methodology is rigorously tested on two balanced radial distribution systems–IEEE 33 bus RDS and APSPDCL 28 bus practical system–evaluating key performance metrics such as power loss and voltage profiles. Simulation results validate the effectiveness of the proposed approach in enhancing system resilience and accommodating the growing penetration of EVs and renewable energy sources. This work provides a practical framework for distribution system operators to address the challenges of integrating EV infrastructure with renewable DGs, contributing to the sustainable evolution of modern power networks. © 2025 Engineers Australia.},
